Question 4/20 – Jenkins + Docker + Kubernetes deployment troubleshooting
Advanced troubleshooting
Scenario:

Your Jenkins pipeline has four stages:
1. Git checkout
2. Docker build
3. Docker push
4. Kubernetes deployment
Your Docker image:
aishwaryalaxmi0599/proj3:latest


Yesterday, you updated your Flask application code and Jenkins successfully built and pushed the latest image.

However, when users access your application, they still see the old version.

Your Kubernetes Deployment shows:
NAME              READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE
proj3-deployment  2/2     2            2


Answer these:
1. What could be the reason Kubernetes is still running the old application version?
A: deployment might not have updated the latest image because when image gets updated in hub then automatically k8s does not build new pods from it. we need to trigger
   the new pod creation by kubectl set image deployment/proj3-deployment proj3-container=aishwaryalaxmi0599/proj3:v2 -n web-apps command. now image is updated and
   new pods are created. verify using: kubectl rollout status deployment/proj3-deployment -n web-apps command

2. What's the problem with repeatedly using the latest tag in Docker?
A: k8s can push new deployment under the same tag thats not the problem but if we do that then k8s might not recognise  that the image content is changed and we also
   cannot identify which version the application is on. so its best to use diff tags like v1, v2 etc

3. How would you verify which Docker image your running Pods are actually using?
A: kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n web-apps (OR) kubectl get pods -n web-apps -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].image}'

4. What changes would you make to your Jenkins pipeline to ensure the latest application version is deployed reliably?
A: build a uniquely tagged docker image: docker build -t aishwaryalaxmi0599/proj3:${BUILD_NUMBER} .
   push this tagged image: docker push aishwaryalaxmi0599/proj3:${BUILD_NUMBER}
   update the k8s deployment using this tag: kubectl set image deployment/proj3-deployment proj3-container=aishwaryalaxmi0599/proj3:$(BUILD_NUMBER) -n web-apps
   wait for rollout to complete: kubectl rollout status deployment proj3-deployment -n web-apps

5. Suppose the new deployment causes application errors. How would you roll back to the previous working version?
A: First check deployment history: kubectl rollout history deployment/proj3-deployment -n web-apps 
   to roll back to previous version: kubectl rollout undo deployment/proj3-deployment -n web-apps
   to roll back to a specified version: kubectl rollout undo deployment/proj3-deployment --to-version=2 -n web-apps 
   verify : kubectl rollout status deployment/proj3-deployment -n web-apps
   
Bonus: What's the difference between kubectl apply and kubectl rollout restart?
A: apply : creates and updates the resources based on manifest file, command: kubectl apply -f dep.yaml
   rollout restart : restarts pods managed by that deployment, command: kubectl rollout restart deployment/proj3-deployment -n web-apps

